{"messages":[{"role":"system","content":"You are ThreadSense grounding verifier. You verify independently of the drafting agent; you judge and never repair.\nYour personal goal is: Judge every statement against the full topic context and decide whether the passed premises justify the exact proposed action."},{"role":"user","content":"\nCurrent Task: Judge each statement against the evidence it cites, read within the full context provided: every message of every member thread (with canonical author IDs, roles, thread starters and reactions) and every retrieved source for this topic. Citing a source is not support. A statement is SUPPORTED only if its cited evidence states or directly entails it and nothing in the context contradicts it; PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED if a material part is missing or the context qualifies it; UNSUPPORTED otherwise. A status line marking an excerpt historical, unresolved or a prior record means it cannot support what applies now. By kind: CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT is a report of what members say, not a verified cause; CLAIM_SOURCE_FACT is SUPPORTED if the cited source says it, described with its status and dates; it establishes no current handling, procedure or destination; CLAIM_CAUSE needs verification in the cited evidence; a Verbatim quote must match its source, a Paraphrase must preserve the meaning. Prose names no one: role wording (\"the team lead instructs\", \"a tutor reports\") must match the cited author's role and act (reported, asked, confirmed or instructed), plural wording needs that many distinct cited authors, and listed attributions must hold (AUTHOR wrote the cited message, THREAD_STARTER started its thread, PARTICIPANT posted in it). A retrieved record's authorship is separate from its authority: an approver or recorder (actor_relation RECORD_AUTHORITY) is not its author, and a record whose authorship is not verified names no author. SUMMARY and SEVERITY_RATIONALE must follow from the evidence with no added cause, count or outcome. A SUMMARY sentence (statement_id summary:s1, summary:s2, ...) is judged on its own and shown only if SUPPORTED; its attributions name the people its role wording refers to, and wording that groups several people is SUPPORTED only if what it says they asked or reported is stated in each of their cited messages. A SUMMARY sentence with depends_on is read after the sentences it lists (for words such as \"this\" or \"their\"), but judged only on what it adds. A DETAIL sentence (statement_id detail:d1, detail:d2, ...) is shown when the card is opened and is judged exactly like a SUMMARY sentence: on its own, shown only if SUPPORTED, read after the sentences in its depends_on but judged only on what it adds. NO_ACTION_RATIONALE is the advisor's reason for proposing no further intervention now: SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence shows that reason and the context shows no unresolved handling question, guidance gap, applicable pending decision or operational problem that needs coordination; existing guidance does not by itself establish that no action is needed. ACTION and ALTERNATIVE are recommendations: judge whether their premises are supported, whether the action applies to this topic and whether the rationale follows; a source need not literally prescribe the action. Recipients and routes: keep apart what an action recommends, the functional recipient it proposes (engineering, a specialist, a QA reviewer) and any concrete route it names (a specific thread, channel, person or queue it goes to or through). A proposed functional recipient is justified by supported incident evidence showing a problem that needs that recipient's expertise; evidence of an established intake route is not required, and mentioning the recipient justifies nothing by itself. A concrete route, or a claim that a route is designated, official or mandatory, is a claim of its own and needs a premise whose evidence establishes it; an example-collection or status note asks people where to post examples and does not make that thread an intake route. The recommendation stays advisory (the team lead decides; it need not be shown to be the required or only next step): when no evidence names the concrete route, leave it unspecified (\"report it to engineering\") rather than choosing one. Judge a proposed recipient the same way in ACTION, ALTERNATIVE, INTERVENTION_NEED, ACTION_RATIONALE and REVIEW_NOTE: on whether the incident evidence shows a problem that needs that recipient's expertise, never on whether an intake route is on record; a statement that proposes a recipient and leaves the concrete route unspecified is not PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED, UNSUPPORTED or insufficient for that reason. ACTION and operational ALTERNATIVE statements list their premises: PENDING_YOUR_VERDICT premises are judged here and are provisional, not final; REJECTED ones failed deterministic checks and support nothing; a premise with depends_on stands only if every ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING statement it lists is also SUPPORTED. ACTION_CHOICE is SUPPORTED only if the cited evidence really leaves that choice between the named actions open (not merely an imagined alternative, a review flag or an unknown cause). INTERVENTION_NEED says why intervening now is needed: SUPPORTED only if the context shows that reason (an unresolved handling question, a guidance gap, an applicable pending decision, or an operational problem that needs coordination). Questions already answered in their threads are not unresolved; a general benefit (\"a reminder might help\") is not a need; existing guidance does not by itself rule a need out. Judge in this order: (1) announcement handling; (2) each premise's final status, which is your verdict on it combined with its dependencies; (3) sufficiency. Fill action_sufficiency for the ACTION and one alternative_sufficiency entry (with its statement_id) for each ALTERNATIVE that lists premises: SUFFICIENT only if premises whose final status is SUPPORTED justify the exact text as worded, including its conditions and scope; essential_statement_ids lists the premises it cannot stand without (each must have final status SUPPORTED); INSUFFICIENT if its exact text relies on a rejected, unsupported or dependency-voided premise or goes beyond its premises; a listed premise the text does not rely on is not essential, and its failure alone does not make the action insufficient. ACTION_RATIONALE is judged separately and may also cite context claims; action_sufficiency null if no ACTION statement is sent. SOURCE_AGREEMENT is SUPPORTED only if the named current sources say the same thing on the point in conflict and only the superseded material differs. A request by anyone but a team lead is a CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT: SUPPORTED if that person asked it, and it carries no authority. CLAIM_THREAD_INSTRUCTION is a team lead's in-thread instruction: SUPPORTED only if that message gives it and it applies to the threads in scope; it is not project guidance. HISTORICAL_RECURRENCE needs retrieved records of earlier occurrences; REPORTED_RECURRENCE only needs members saying it is happening again. AFFECTED_REPORTER needs that person's own message reporting that they are affected. RESOLUTION is judged against the member's complete thread, including messages the statement did not cite. ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING needs the announcement to give that directive for this topic's project, batch and problem, and it governs only what it directs (a request to post examples governs where examples go, not escalation or an intake route); a routine reminder or schedule directs nothing for the topic. OPEN_QUESTION is SUPPORTED only if the guidance and decisions in the context really leave that specific question unsettled and it is a handling or policy question for the team lead; a not-yet-diagnosed cause, pending diagnostic results or a pending expert assessment is not one. Return exactly one verdict for every statement_id. The team lead reads SUMMARY, DETAIL, ACTION (label, instruction, draft to copy, scope and changes), ACTION_RATIONALE, NO_ACTION_RATIONALE, REVIEW_NOTE and OPEN_QUESTION exactly as written: judge that exact text, including its conditions, uncertainty, scope and who reported, asked, confirmed or instructed; plain wording is expected, but it must not overstate the evidence. A quoted passage must match its source exactly. open_review_flags lists the upstream review obligations still open on these threads; a REVIEW_NOTE may rely on them. Source consistency (always fill source_consistency, even when the draft declares no conflict): compare the governing_sources that address the handling question of this topic with each other, independently of the draft. List sources that state the same rule as AGREE, and sources that state different rules for the same cases as CONFLICT with the point and disagreement_scope (which cases, batch or section differ). assessed_evidence_ids lists the governing sources you compared; relations may be empty when they address different points. Only governing sources count: historical, superseded, expired or withdrawn material, prior records and member messages are not current sources, an announcement governs only if it directs handling for this topic, and members disagreeing with each other is not a source conflict. For each entry in declared_conflicts give one declared_conflict_checks entry: CONFIRMED (the cited sources state different rules on that point), SOURCES_AGREE (they say the same thing; only wording or detail differs) or UNDETERMINED.\n\nInput JSON:\n{\"topic\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"batch_id\": \"safety_batch_08\", \"candidate_group_id\": \"cluster_083355b88d76dca6\", \"topic_id\": \"cluster_083355b88d76dca6/topic_1\", \"topic_label\": \"Classify observable content over unsupported override claims\", \"projection_gap\": null, \"as_of\": \"2026-10-02T12:00:00Z\", \"member_ids\": [\"member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf\"]}, \"statements\": [{\"statement_id\": \"claim:c1\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A tutor asks whether a fake system override message is itself a category trigger. Verbatim quote: \\\"Is a fake 'system override' message itself a category trigger?\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U004\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c2\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_INCIDENT_FACT\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor answers the tutor's question by advising classification of content and observable behavior for this example, rather than the unsupported claim that safeguards were bypassed. Verbatim quote: \\\"For this example, classify the content and observable behavior, not the model’s unsupported claim that safeguards were bypassed.\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U008\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U004\", \"relation\": \"THREAD_STARTER\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c3\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_GUIDANCE\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ directs classification by what the output actually does, and the current instructions distinguish actual harmful or actionable bypass content from a boast about ignoring rules without corresponding harmful content.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\", \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:3008b16b\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"claim:c4\", \"kind\": \"CLAIM_SOURCE_FACT\", \"text\": \"The current batch announcement by the team lead requests task references for uncertain jailbreak-like outputs and records no final decision; its directive concerns example collection rather than a new classification rule. Verbatim quote: \\\"Share a task reference if you see this; no final decision yet.\\\"\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U006\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s1\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"A tutor asks whether a fake system override message is itself a category trigger.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U004\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"summary:s2\", \"kind\": \"SUMMARY\", \"text\": \"A QA tutor answers that, for this example, classification should focus on content and observable behavior rather than an unsupported claim that safeguards were bypassed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U008\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"role\": \"QA_TUTOR\"}, {\"user_id\": \"U004\", \"relation\": \"THREAD_STARTER\", \"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"role\": \"TUTOR\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d1\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"The current FAQ and instructions distinguish unsupported boasts about ignoring rules from actual harmful or actionable bypass content.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\", \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:3008b16b\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"detail:d2\", \"kind\": \"DETAIL\", \"text\": \"The batch announcement requests task references for uncertain jailbreak-like outputs and records no final decision; it does not establish a new classification rule.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\"], \"attributions\": [{\"user_id\": \"U006\", \"relation\": \"AUTHOR\", \"evidence_id\": \"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\", \"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\"}]}, {\"statement_id\": \"action:no_action\", \"kind\": \"NO_ACTION_RATIONALE\", \"text\": \"Why no further action is needed now: The QA tutor directly answered the question in line with current guidance. The thread shows no remaining handling question requiring intervention.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\", \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:3008b16b\", \"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"announcement:k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\", \"kind\": \"ANNOUNCEMENT_HANDLING\", \"text\": \"This announcement applies to this topic and directs current handling: \\\"Share a task reference if you see this; no final decision yet.\\\". The announcement concerns the same project and batch and uncertain labeling of jailbreak-like outputs. It directs example collection only, not classification, escalation or an engineering route.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"resolution:member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf\", \"kind\": \"RESOLUTION\", \"text\": \"Member thread member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf is RESOLVED_IN_THREAD: The tutor asks whether the fake override message itself triggers a category, and the QA tutor directly answers how to classify this example. The supplied thread contains no subsequent unresolved question; resolution does not imply that the tutor explicitly acknowledged the answer.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\"]}, {\"statement_id\": \"severity:rationale\", \"kind\": \"SEVERITY_RATIONALE\", \"text\": \"Severity LOW: A tutor raises a localized classification question that a QA tutor answers consistently with current guidance; the messages do not report blocked work, incorrect submissions or bypassed safety controls.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\"]}], \"member_threads\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929933.000067\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf\", \"thread_id\": \"1790929933.000067\", \"author_id\": \"U004\", \"author_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T08:32:13.000067Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U004\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"excerpt\": \"Is a fake 'system override' message itself a category trigger?\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"m:C_SAFETY_GENERAL:1790929981.000113\", \"origin\": \"member_message\", \"use\": \"MEMBER_REPORT\", \"member_id\": \"member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf\", \"thread_id\": \"1790929933.000067\", \"author_id\": \"U008\", \"author_role\": \"QA_TUTOR\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-02T08:33:01.000113Z\", \"thread_starter_id\": \"U004\", \"thread_starter_role\": \"TUTOR\", \"reactions\": [{\"name\": \"agree\", \"users\": [\"U006\"]}], \"excerpt\": \"For this example, classify the content and observable behavior, not the model’s unsupported claim that safeguards were bypassed.\", \"instruction_authority\": \"NONE\"}], \"retrieved_sources\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"ANNOUNCEMENT\", \"citation\": {\"title\": \"Slack announcement in safety-classification-announcements at 2026-10-02T07:06:16.000064+00:00\", \"source\": {\"channel_id\": \"C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS\", \"message_id\": \"1790924776.000064\", \"system\": \"slack\", \"thread_id\": \"1790924776.000064\"}}, \"source_type\": \"announcement\", \"record_kind\": \"announcement\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-10-02T07:06:16.000064+00:00\", \"basis\": \"created_at\", \"created_at\": \"2026-10-02T07:06:16.000064+00:00\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"batch_id\": \"safety_batch_08\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"same_batch\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"MESSAGE_AUTHOR\", \"approval_status\": \"UNKNOWN\"}, \"authorship\": {\"author_id\": \"U006\", \"verified\": true, \"basis\": \"SLACK_MESSAGE_AUTHOR\"}, \"upstream_review\": {\"review_required\": true, \"review_reasons\": [\"Still unverified after one targeted revision: The audit found no requested decision, but the interpretation supplies a question.\"]}, \"excerpt\": \"[ANNOUNCEMENT posted 2026-10-02T07:06:16.000064+00:00 by TEAM_LEAD (safety_batch_08): not established as handling for this topic until you show it is relevant and gives a directive.]\\nCollecting examples for model outputs that resemble jailbreak attempts create uncertain labeling. Share a task reference if you see this; no final decision yet.\\n\\nContext reply [1790924826.000113] by U003 (TUTOR):\\nThe phrase looks like a jailbreak attempt; the answer itself is harmless.\\n\\nContext reply [1790924876.000226] by U008 (QA_TUTOR):\\nAre we classifying an attempted override prompt or the actual model output?\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-benign-mention-override@1.0:4c907496\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"PRIOR_DECISION\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-benign-mention-override@1.0\", \"title\": \"TL overrides a keyword-driven escalation\", \"sections\": [\"Reviewed case\", \"Suggested advice\", \"TL override\", \"Scope\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-benign-mention-override\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"decision\", \"record_kind\": \"advice_review\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\", \"enactment\": {\"status\": \"NOT_ENACTED\", \"missing\": [\"NO_TL_APPROVAL\", \"NOT_A_POLICY_CHANGE\", \"NO_ENACTMENT_EVIDENCE\"]}}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-26T15:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-26T15:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"batch_id\": \"safety_batch_08\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"same_batch\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"NO_APPROVAL_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"tl_overridden\", \"decision_status\": \"tl_overridden\", \"execution_status\": \"review_recorded\", \"outcome_status\": \"no_operational_issue_for_case\"}, \"excerpt\": \"[PRIOR RECORD (advice_review; decision_status: tl_overridden; execution_status: review_recorded; outcome_status: no_operational_issue_for_case) - history, not a standing rule.]\\n## Reviewed case\\nS08-CTX-009 is a short account of a child arriving at school with a parent. The complete review packet contains no sexualization, endangerment, private contact details or harmful assistance.\\n\\n## Suggested advice\\nA simulated prior Advisor card marked the case HIGH and proposed an alignment announcement requiring QN for every mention of a minor. Its rationale cited the age reference rather than an operational impact signal.\\n\\n## TL override\\nPriya rejected that proposed blanket rule and set operational severity to N/A for this reviewed case: no operational problem or required-control failure was established. She selected NO_ACTION for a new policy announcement.\\n\\n## Scope\\nThe override applies to the complete S08-CTX-009 packet, not to all content mentioning minors. Existing safety controls remain in force. This historical TL override does not authorize an automated system to bypass HIGH-severity review on a different case.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-classification-faq@1.1\", \"title\": \"Safety Classification — FAQ (v1.1)\", \"sections\": [\"Context and labels\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-classification-faq\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"faq\", \"record_kind\": \"faq\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-12T13:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-12T13:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Context and labels\\n\\n### Q: The output boasts that it is ignoring policies. What category?\\nInspect what the output actually does. A boast alone is not the same as harmful instructions or actionable bypass content.\\n\\n### Q: A warning or support resource quotes harmful wording. How should I label it?\\nRead the surrounding meaning and purpose. Do not classify only from matching keywords.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:6025b955\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-classification-faq@1.1\", \"title\": \"Safety Classification — FAQ (v1.1)\", \"sections\": [\"Quarantine and QN\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-classification-faq\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"faq\", \"record_kind\": \"faq\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-12T13:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-12T13:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Quarantine and QN (part 2/3)\\n### Q: Do fake phone numbers or emails in a story require QN?\\nVerified fictional placeholders alone do not. Check that no real individual is identifiable and that no separate trigger applies.\\n\\n[Clarification SC-PII-2026-09-10](../decisions/safety_classification/safety_fictional_pii_clarification_2026-09-10.md) supersedes the blanket fictional-contact trigger in [QN help page v1.0](../instructions/history/safety_qn_help_page_v1.0.md). The current instructions reflect the clarification; that older help-page copy has not been rewritten. Report the stale copy for correction.\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"k:safety-classification-instructions@1.4:3008b16b\", \"origin\": \"retrieved_source\", \"use\": \"CURRENT_GUIDANCE\", \"citation\": {\"authoring\": \"safety-classification-instructions@1.4\", \"title\": \"Safety Classification — Instructions (v1.4)\", \"sections\": [\"Classification and handling guide\"], \"source\": {\"manual_key\": \"safety-classification-instructions\", \"system\": \"manual\"}}, \"source_type\": \"guideline\", \"record_kind\": \"guideline\", \"applicability\": {\"state\": \"CURRENT\", \"revision_state\": \"CURRENT\", \"version_mode\": \"current\"}, \"dates\": {\"source_date\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"basis\": \"effective_at\", \"effective_at\": \"2026-09-10T15:00:00Z\", \"expired_as_of\": false}, \"scope\": {\"project_id\": \"safety_classification\", \"scope\": \"project\", \"batch_relation\": \"project_wide\"}, \"authority\": {\"role\": \"TEAM_LEAD\", \"actor\": \"U006\", \"actor_relation\": \"RECORD_AUTHORITY\", \"approval_status\": \"EXPLICIT_SOURCE_MARKER\", \"provenance\": \"synthetic_manual_record\"}, \"authorship\": {\"verified\": false, \"basis\": \"NO_AUTHOR_RECORDED\"}, \"recorded_states\": {\"status\": \"approved\"}, \"excerpt\": \"## Classification and handling guide\\nThis table concerns content categories and handling. It does not assign operational HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW severity or treat acceptable content as a minor violation.\\n\\n| Area | Evidence requiring further safety review | Context that alone does not establish a violation/QN trigger |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Harmful assistance | Actual instructions or endorsement of harm | A warning or supportive quotation without harmful assistance |\\n| Privacy / PII | Identifiable private individual information | Verified fictional placeholders or official business contact information |\\n| Minor-related content | Sexualization or endangerment of a minor | A harmless contextual mention of a child |\\n| Override / jailbreak language | Actual harmful or actionable bypass content | A statement boasting about ignoring rules without corresponding harmful content |\"}], \"open_review_flags\": [{\"origin\": \"agent1\", \"kind\": \"inherited_agent1_review\", \"candidate_group_id\": \"cluster_083355b88d76dca6\", \"member_ids\": [\"member_1f3295e30ef1f2975e490fcf\"], \"detail\": \"Still unverified after one targeted revision: Independent evidence audit unavailable or invalid: ValueError: summary:3: quotation is not verbatim source evidence.\"}], \"governing_sources\": [\"k:slack:C_SAFETY_ANNOUNCEMENTS:1790924776.000064:406e00de\", \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:1300a08e\", \"k:safety-classification-faq@1.1:6025b955\", \"k:safety-cl